<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cyclone Nargis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2008">2008</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cyclone Nargis was a tropical cyclone that struck Myanmar on 2–3 May 2008, driving a storm surge across the Irrawaddy delta. About 138,000 people were killed or went missing, the worst natural disaster in the country's recorded history. The military government at first restricted foreign aid and relief workers, and held its constitutional referendum days later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyclone-nargis.jpg" alt="Cyclone Nargis" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nargis_2008-05-02_0645Z.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Towards the end of April 2008 the Inter-Tropical Convergence Zone over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> became very active, and a tropical disturbance developed on 25 April. The India Meteorological Department classified it as a depression on 27 April, and on 28 April it was upgraded to Cyclonic Storm Nargis, about 550 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>. It was the first named storm of the 2008 North Indian Ocean cyclone season. Nargis first moved slowly northwest, weakened briefly in dry air on 29 April, then turned east and intensified rapidly. Indian authorities are reported to have warned Myanmar of the danger 48 hours before landfall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Nargis made landfall in the Ayeyarwady Division on 2 May 2008 at peak intensity, with winds of at least 165 km/h by Indian observations and 215 km/h by the Joint Typhoon Warning Center, which rated it a weak Category 4 cyclone. It passed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a> and then weakened until it dissipated near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. The storm surge ran 40 kilometres up the densely populated Irrawaddy delta. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> estimated that 1.5 million people were severely affected, and damage was put at 13 trillion kyat (US$15.3 billion).</p><p>Confirmed dead numbered 84,537 and 53,836 were missing, with 19,359 injured, giving a total of 138,373. Labutta Township alone was reported to have 80,000 dead and Bogale about 10,000. Critics of the military government argue that the figures were under-reported, and some aid workers and organisations estimated far higher tolls. The feared second wave of deaths from disease and lack of relief did not materialise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Relief was slowed for political reasons. On 6 May the government asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> for help but remained resistant, preferring bilateral aid, and visas were the biggest obstacle for aid workers waiting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a> airport.</p><p>On 9 May it declared that it would accept food, medicines and money but not foreign aid workers or military units. The referendum on the constitution went ahead on 10 May, with voting postponed to 24 May in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a> and the worst hit areas. More than two weeks after the storm, relief had reached only 25 percent of the people in need. The military government finally accepted aid a few days after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s offer was taken up.</p><p>Nargis is regarded as the deadliest named cyclone ever recorded. At the time it was also the costliest tropical cyclone in the North Indian Ocean, a record broken by Amphan in 2020.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cyclone Nargis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The 2008 constitution, passed in a disputed referendum days after <b>Cyclone Nargis</b>, reserved a quarter of parliamentary seats and key ministries for the military.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
